WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.910 --> 00:00:06.160
MICHAEL BALDWIN: Hi, everyone.

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:07.420
I'm Michael Baldwin.

00:00:07.420 --> 00:00:09.520
I lead Financial Services
Product Management

00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:10.810
for Google Cloud.

00:00:10.810 --> 00:00:13.330
With me today is my
colleague Tom Thane, also

00:00:13.330 --> 00:00:15.370
a financial services
product manager.

00:00:15.370 --> 00:00:17.020
Thanks for joining us.

00:00:17.020 --> 00:00:19.060
Tom and I both spend
our days thinking

00:00:19.060 --> 00:00:22.390
about how we can help financial
institutions transform

00:00:22.390 --> 00:00:24.880
their businesses with artificial
intelligence and machine

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:25.810
learning.

00:00:25.810 --> 00:00:28.030
Today, we'll be sharing
with you an overview

00:00:28.030 --> 00:00:29.590
of some of the
major areas where we

00:00:29.590 --> 00:00:31.360
see this transformation
happening

00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:34.420
and how Google Cloud can help.

00:00:34.420 --> 00:00:35.920
We know that business
transformation

00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:39.550
is top of mind for many of our
financial services customers.

00:00:39.550 --> 00:00:41.440
However, these are
challenging times,

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:43.690
with financial services
institutions navigating

00:00:43.690 --> 00:00:44.950
the pandemic.

00:00:44.950 --> 00:00:47.080
Interest rates are
at an all-time low.

00:00:47.080 --> 00:00:49.450
Customer expectations are
changing with the shift

00:00:49.450 --> 00:00:50.530
to digital.

00:00:50.530 --> 00:00:55.030
For example, digital banking
has increased from 63% in 2019

00:00:55.030 --> 00:00:57.100
to 72% today.

00:00:57.100 --> 00:00:59.310
Regulatory requirements
are evolving

00:00:59.310 --> 00:01:01.690
and compliance costs
are increasing.

00:01:01.690 --> 00:01:04.000
Also, the financial
services industry as a whole

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:07.000
is facing new and
creative types of fraud.

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:08.890
For example, the dollar
value of attempted

00:01:08.890 --> 00:01:13.310
fraudulent transactions grew
35% in April year-over-year.

00:01:13.310 --> 00:01:15.640
And finally, with
the move to digital,

00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:17.470
disruption is accelerating.

00:01:17.470 --> 00:01:20.590
For instance, the pandemic
drove a 72% increase

00:01:20.590 --> 00:01:24.360
in the use of fintech
apps in Europe.

00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:25.860
Today, we'll cover
three areas where

00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:28.860
we see AI helping to unlock
business transformation

00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:30.690
in financial services.

00:01:30.690 --> 00:01:34.440
First, AI can help reimagine
the customer experience

00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:37.440
to deliver things such as
personalized digital banking,

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:40.310
contact centers with a
AI-powered virtual agents,

00:01:40.310 --> 00:01:43.740
and smoother customer
experiences for traditionally

00:01:43.740 --> 00:01:46.840
paper-intensive
processes, like lending.

00:01:46.840 --> 00:01:49.480
Second, financial institutions
are looking for ways

00:01:49.480 --> 00:01:51.640
to use data and analytics
to transform how

00:01:51.640 --> 00:01:53.870
they detect and manage risk.

00:01:53.870 --> 00:01:56.290
And finally, financial
institutions ask us

00:01:56.290 --> 00:02:00.340
for a best-in-class AI platform
that is flexible, secure,

00:02:00.340 --> 00:02:02.380
and compliant,
that empowers them

00:02:02.380 --> 00:02:05.050
to build their own
AI-powered solutions

00:02:05.050 --> 00:02:08.490
to solve their most
critical business problems.

00:02:08.490 --> 00:02:10.139
As we talk about
through examples

00:02:10.139 --> 00:02:13.230
of AI-powered transformation
in financial services,

00:02:13.230 --> 00:02:15.690
you'll see that AI isn't
a science experiment.

00:02:15.690 --> 00:02:18.030
It's being used to solve
real-world problems

00:02:18.030 --> 00:02:22.110
across banking, capital markets,
insurance, and payments.

00:02:22.110 --> 00:02:24.740
The key thing for us is
working with our customers

00:02:24.740 --> 00:02:27.960
to identify their top business
problems, then figuring out

00:02:27.960 --> 00:02:31.050
how AI can be most helpful
for them in solving

00:02:31.050 --> 00:02:32.910
for their key needs.

00:02:32.910 --> 00:02:36.210
And ultimately, our goal is
to make it as easy as possible

00:02:36.210 --> 00:02:39.090
to deploy these solutions
in real-world, end-to-end

00:02:39.090 --> 00:02:40.830
workflows.

00:02:40.830 --> 00:02:44.700
Deloitte recently surveyed
over 2,700 global IT

00:02:44.700 --> 00:02:48.360
and line-of-business executives
to obtain a global view of how

00:02:48.360 --> 00:02:52.080
organizations are adopting,
benefiting from, and managing

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:53.790
AI technologies.

00:02:53.790 --> 00:02:56.370
And as you can see from this
recent Deloitte survey, shown

00:02:56.370 --> 00:02:59.130
here on the slide,
81% of customers

00:02:59.130 --> 00:03:03.180
are reporting payback on their
AI in less than two years.

00:03:03.180 --> 00:03:05.390
When AI is absorbed
in this way, it

00:03:05.390 --> 00:03:08.970
can lead to true
business transformation.

00:03:08.970 --> 00:03:11.220
So first, let's talk about
how artificial intelligence

00:03:11.220 --> 00:03:14.940
can help transform the
customer experience.

00:03:14.940 --> 00:03:17.430
Customer experience is,
obviously, a broad area.

00:03:17.430 --> 00:03:20.670
Let's talk through a few
examples of what this can mean.

00:03:20.670 --> 00:03:22.200
Many financial
institutions today

00:03:22.200 --> 00:03:25.110
struggle to get a single view
of the customer, sometimes known

00:03:25.110 --> 00:03:27.630
as customer 360.

00:03:27.630 --> 00:03:31.050
With the continued shift from
physical branches to digital,

00:03:31.050 --> 00:03:33.300
financial institutions
are looking for new ways

00:03:33.300 --> 00:03:36.750
to deliver high-quality and
helpful experiences across all

00:03:36.750 --> 00:03:39.120
of their customer touch
points, lighting up

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:43.350
omnichannel operations across
web, mobile, contact centers,

00:03:43.350 --> 00:03:45.810
physical branches, and ATMs.

00:03:45.810 --> 00:03:47.970
When it comes to
acquiring new customers,

00:03:47.970 --> 00:03:51.510
financial institutions want
to onboard in days, not weeks,

00:03:51.510 --> 00:03:54.000
and are looking to use
AI-powered intelligence

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:56.440
to help retain those customers.

00:03:56.440 --> 00:03:59.490
Finally, as customer
360 becomes a reality,

00:03:59.490 --> 00:04:02.250
we'll be able to enable
AI-powered experiences

00:04:02.250 --> 00:04:04.950
to sell the right product
to the right customer

00:04:04.950 --> 00:04:07.710
at the right time,
and also drive

00:04:07.710 --> 00:04:10.920
other personalized insights to
help with customer retention

00:04:10.920 --> 00:04:14.480
and new business acquisition.

00:04:14.480 --> 00:04:17.420
Our Contact Center AI
solution is one example

00:04:17.420 --> 00:04:21.320
of how AI is helping reimagine
the customer experience.

00:04:21.320 --> 00:04:24.830
First, let me share a quick
overview of our Contact Center

00:04:24.830 --> 00:04:26.220
AI solution.

00:04:26.220 --> 00:04:29.420
The goal of Contact Center AI is
to deliver exceptional customer

00:04:29.420 --> 00:04:32.090
service and increase
operational efficiency

00:04:32.090 --> 00:04:34.070
using artificial intelligence.

00:04:34.070 --> 00:04:36.500
It enables virtual agents
to converse naturally

00:04:36.500 --> 00:04:39.380
with customers and expertly
assist human agents

00:04:39.380 --> 00:04:41.150
on complex cases.

00:04:41.150 --> 00:04:44.370
It does this through a set of
underlying powerful features,

00:04:44.370 --> 00:04:47.750
for example, Dialogflow, which
automates basic chat and voice

00:04:47.750 --> 00:04:50.210
interactions, a feature
called Agent Assist,

00:04:50.210 --> 00:04:52.310
which makes human
agents more effective,

00:04:52.310 --> 00:04:55.190
and Insights, which unlocks
insights about call drivers

00:04:55.190 --> 00:04:57.560
and customer service
interactions.

00:04:57.560 --> 00:05:01.100
Customer support is particularly
relevant in financial services,

00:05:01.100 --> 00:05:04.850
as financial institutions
have seen 1,000% increase

00:05:04.850 --> 00:05:07.820
in call volume since the
onset of the pandemic.

00:05:07.820 --> 00:05:09.650
Customers are calling
in with questions

00:05:09.650 --> 00:05:11.870
like are branches still open?

00:05:11.870 --> 00:05:13.550
Can I defer loan payments?

00:05:13.550 --> 00:05:16.740
Are you waiving
any fees right now?

00:05:16.740 --> 00:05:18.390
And, of course,
security is paramount

00:05:18.390 --> 00:05:19.900
in financial services.

00:05:19.900 --> 00:05:22.260
So our teams are hard
at work making sure

00:05:22.260 --> 00:05:26.580
that we have bank-level security
built into this product.

00:05:26.580 --> 00:05:28.410
Next, I want to share
how we're working

00:05:28.410 --> 00:05:30.030
to make lending
more operationally

00:05:30.030 --> 00:05:33.150
efficient and create smoother
customer experiences.

00:05:33.150 --> 00:05:36.720
Our Lending Document AI alpha is
built on top of our Document AI

00:05:36.720 --> 00:05:38.070
technology.

00:05:38.070 --> 00:05:41.880
Document AI helps
organizations unlock insights

00:05:41.880 --> 00:05:44.010
from their mountains
of documents using

00:05:44.010 --> 00:05:47.250
artificial intelligence by
turning unstructured data

00:05:47.250 --> 00:05:49.860
into structured data, which
means you can now actually

00:05:49.860 --> 00:05:52.200
understand and utilize it.

00:05:52.200 --> 00:05:54.360
With mortgage rates
low, there's a surge

00:05:54.360 --> 00:05:56.310
in mortgage applications.

00:05:56.310 --> 00:05:59.610
This solution provides a bundle
of specialized models, focused

00:05:59.610 --> 00:06:02.350
on specific document
types used in lending.

00:06:02.350 --> 00:06:04.860
And with this solution, we're
able to work with lenders

00:06:04.860 --> 00:06:07.260
to quickly onboard
customers and speed up

00:06:07.260 --> 00:06:09.360
the processing of
paper-intensive mortgage

00:06:09.360 --> 00:06:11.010
applications.

00:06:11.010 --> 00:06:13.680
Also, when the US Congress
authorized the Paycheck

00:06:13.680 --> 00:06:16.860
Protection Program as part of
the pandemic relief package,

00:06:16.860 --> 00:06:18.930
we made this same
technology available to help

00:06:18.930 --> 00:06:23.550
with the processing
of PPP loans.

00:06:23.550 --> 00:06:25.860
Next, let's talk
about insurance.

00:06:25.860 --> 00:06:27.390
Dealing with a car
accident can be

00:06:27.390 --> 00:06:29.610
a major headache for
the automobile owner

00:06:29.610 --> 00:06:32.190
and a time-consuming
process for the insurer.

00:06:32.190 --> 00:06:36.510
We worked with USAA, an insurer,
and a Google partner, Mitchell,

00:06:36.510 --> 00:06:38.580
to build ML models
that analyze images

00:06:38.580 --> 00:06:40.110
of cars that have
been in accidents

00:06:40.110 --> 00:06:42.480
and make damaged
parts estimates.

00:06:42.480 --> 00:06:45.300
This, in turn, can be converted
into an estimate of costs

00:06:45.300 --> 00:06:48.480
needed to repair the car,
saving appraisers' time

00:06:48.480 --> 00:06:50.782
and leading to smoother
customer experience.

00:06:50.782 --> 00:06:52.740
Now I'm going to hand
things over to Tom Shane,

00:06:52.740 --> 00:06:55.760
my colleague and fellow product
manager for financial services,

00:06:55.760 --> 00:06:58.020
who will walk us through
how AI is driving business

00:06:58.020 --> 00:06:59.970
transformation and
risk management,

00:06:59.970 --> 00:07:03.420
and also will share an overview
of how our AI platform is being

00:07:03.420 --> 00:07:05.910
used by developers
and data scientists

00:07:05.910 --> 00:07:09.210
inside financial institutions
to deliver on their top business

00:07:09.210 --> 00:07:10.590
priorities.

00:07:10.590 --> 00:07:13.005
Over to you, Tom.

00:07:13.005 --> 00:07:14.790
TOM SHANE: Thanks, Michael.

00:07:14.790 --> 00:07:16.590
When Google Cloud
thinks about how

00:07:16.590 --> 00:07:19.860
we can help apply AI
and ML to transform

00:07:19.860 --> 00:07:22.360
financial services and
the risk management space,

00:07:22.360 --> 00:07:24.970
we typically think
about four key things.

00:07:24.970 --> 00:07:27.690
First is mitigating
risk across channels

00:07:27.690 --> 00:07:29.460
and digital experiences.

00:07:29.460 --> 00:07:31.230
As Michael said,
financial institutions

00:07:31.230 --> 00:07:35.160
are increasingly looking to
move to a digital-first posture

00:07:35.160 --> 00:07:37.260
in the post-COVID environment.

00:07:37.260 --> 00:07:39.750
That provides great benefits
from a customer experience

00:07:39.750 --> 00:07:40.660
perspective.

00:07:40.660 --> 00:07:44.580
But it does open up new vectors
of risk and potential fraud,

00:07:44.580 --> 00:07:48.450
for example, rising scams in
the retail banking segment.

00:07:48.450 --> 00:07:52.770
Second, moving to holistic
and contextual analysis--

00:07:52.770 --> 00:07:55.440
by holistic, we mean
extracting signals

00:07:55.440 --> 00:07:57.360
from traditional
structured data sources,

00:07:57.360 --> 00:08:01.110
but also combining that with
insights from unstructured data

00:08:01.110 --> 00:08:05.130
to form one holistic
or customer 360 view.

00:08:05.130 --> 00:08:07.890
That allows financial
services organizations

00:08:07.890 --> 00:08:11.190
to contextually analyze
customers and events

00:08:11.190 --> 00:08:13.200
and detect true
anomalies or risks that

00:08:13.200 --> 00:08:15.870
need to be reviewed from normal
behavior for that customer

00:08:15.870 --> 00:08:17.340
or event.

00:08:17.340 --> 00:08:21.240
Third, thinking about how to
dynamically and rapidly respond

00:08:21.240 --> 00:08:22.860
to changing risk types--

00:08:22.860 --> 00:08:25.470
for example, changing
macroeconomic and market

00:08:25.470 --> 00:08:29.350
conditions or new fraud types
in the post-COVID world.

00:08:29.350 --> 00:08:32.580
And finally, being able to offer
that worry-free and seamless

00:08:32.580 --> 00:08:34.110
customer experience--

00:08:34.110 --> 00:08:36.690
customers want to know that the
risk management systems that

00:08:36.690 --> 00:08:39.390
are operating in the background
are keeping their accounts

00:08:39.390 --> 00:08:41.280
and assets safe,
but they don't want

00:08:41.280 --> 00:08:44.920
those systems to inappropriately
impact the customer experience.

00:08:44.920 --> 00:08:49.670
For example, a false positive
alert on a fraud transaction.

00:08:49.670 --> 00:08:52.360
Next, I want to talk
about a customer who's

00:08:52.360 --> 00:08:56.500
working to help transform their
risk management processes.

00:08:56.500 --> 00:08:58.630
HSBC is working
with Google Cloud

00:08:58.630 --> 00:09:01.120
to leverage the latest
in machine learning

00:09:01.120 --> 00:09:04.300
to dynamically assess
potential financial crime risk

00:09:04.300 --> 00:09:06.490
and holistically
look at customers.

00:09:06.490 --> 00:09:09.130
HSBC is bringing their
extensive knowledge

00:09:09.130 --> 00:09:11.080
of combating
financial crime, which

00:09:11.080 --> 00:09:14.410
combined with Google's expertise
in developing ML solutions,

00:09:14.410 --> 00:09:16.690
is already leading to
encouraging results

00:09:16.690 --> 00:09:18.730
in this area.

00:09:18.730 --> 00:09:21.930
You may have heard from our
CEO, Thomas Kurian's, keynote

00:09:21.930 --> 00:09:24.370
that Google Cloud is
focused on building up

00:09:24.370 --> 00:09:29.020
digital transformation solutions
powered by Google's AI.

00:09:29.020 --> 00:09:31.180
In risk management,
we're focused

00:09:31.180 --> 00:09:34.640
on giving you a preview of
two of those solutions today.

00:09:34.640 --> 00:09:37.450
The first is anti-money
laundering, or AML,

00:09:37.450 --> 00:09:40.300
and the second is know
your customer, or KYC.

00:09:40.300 --> 00:09:43.300
I want to talk about some of
the challenges and opportunities

00:09:43.300 --> 00:09:45.130
in each of these areas.

00:09:45.130 --> 00:09:47.287
When our customers
ask us about AML,

00:09:47.287 --> 00:09:49.870
they often talk about challenges
around the current rule-based

00:09:49.870 --> 00:09:52.990
system, and that sophisticated
bad actors can sometimes

00:09:52.990 --> 00:09:55.900
get around these systems,
leading to potential missed

00:09:55.900 --> 00:09:57.250
money laundering events.

00:09:57.250 --> 00:10:00.910
And the system often lead
to many false positives.

00:10:00.910 --> 00:10:03.520
On the data side, we also hear
challenges about poor data

00:10:03.520 --> 00:10:08.290
quality and the lack of that
holistic or customer 360 view

00:10:08.290 --> 00:10:11.330
to inform their AML efforts.

00:10:11.330 --> 00:10:13.780
And finally, we hear that
all of these false positives

00:10:13.780 --> 00:10:16.720
are creating heavy amounts
of manual work, which

00:10:16.720 --> 00:10:20.620
is taking away from
higher-value opportunities.

00:10:20.620 --> 00:10:22.740
In terms of opportunities
for innovation,

00:10:22.740 --> 00:10:25.900
Google Cloud can help
extract insights from data

00:10:25.900 --> 00:10:29.800
and apply AI and ML technologies
to better detect and prevent

00:10:29.800 --> 00:10:32.590
and identify those real
money laundering events,

00:10:32.590 --> 00:10:35.260
and then help organizations
operate more efficiently

00:10:35.260 --> 00:10:39.070
by reducing false positives, as
well as providing more insights

00:10:39.070 --> 00:10:41.950
in the alert review process.

00:10:41.950 --> 00:10:44.200
Know your customer, or
KYC, is another area

00:10:44.200 --> 00:10:46.750
that financial institutions
often ask us about.

00:10:46.750 --> 00:10:49.510
We hear challenges,
such as manual processes

00:10:49.510 --> 00:10:52.270
around looking at
documents and combining

00:10:52.270 --> 00:10:53.483
that with other information.

00:10:53.483 --> 00:10:55.900
We hear how this sometimes
impacts the customer onboarding

00:10:55.900 --> 00:10:58.120
process, either
delaying it or leading

00:10:58.120 --> 00:10:59.800
to excessive
outreach to customers

00:10:59.800 --> 00:11:01.198
to collect information.

00:11:01.198 --> 00:11:02.740
And finally, we hear
about challenges

00:11:02.740 --> 00:11:04.750
of integrating all
this information

00:11:04.750 --> 00:11:07.000
into banks' overall systems.

00:11:07.000 --> 00:11:08.770
We think there's three
key opportunities

00:11:08.770 --> 00:11:10.940
to help improve this process.

00:11:10.940 --> 00:11:13.400
The first is helping
in the automation part.

00:11:13.400 --> 00:11:16.180
By automation, we
mean looking at all

00:11:16.180 --> 00:11:18.940
of the documents received
in the KYC process

00:11:18.940 --> 00:11:22.120
and automatically extracting
insights, entities,

00:11:22.120 --> 00:11:24.070
and information from
these documents,

00:11:24.070 --> 00:11:26.500
and putting them into
downstream processes.

00:11:26.500 --> 00:11:28.000
We also think there's
an opportunity

00:11:28.000 --> 00:11:32.050
to move to a rapid and digital
onboarding process, especially

00:11:32.050 --> 00:11:34.430
important in the
post-COVID environment.

00:11:34.430 --> 00:11:37.630
And finally, we think extracting
insights from these documents

00:11:37.630 --> 00:11:41.860
can better identify potential
risk sooner, as well as inform

00:11:41.860 --> 00:11:44.620
financial services
organizations' overall customer

00:11:44.620 --> 00:11:47.050
360 efforts.

00:11:47.050 --> 00:11:49.060
Next, I want to turn
to the capital markets

00:11:49.060 --> 00:11:51.760
space and BNY
Mellon, who's using

00:11:51.760 --> 00:11:55.480
machine learning to predict
treasury settlement failures.

00:11:55.480 --> 00:11:58.090
The treasury market is one of
the largest and most liquid

00:11:58.090 --> 00:11:59.240
in the world.

00:11:59.240 --> 00:12:01.420
However, 1% to 2%
of transactions

00:12:01.420 --> 00:12:05.350
fail every day, which
equates to about $70 billion.

00:12:05.350 --> 00:12:07.990
So BNY Mellon is
leveraging Google Cloud

00:12:07.990 --> 00:12:11.230
to develop end-to-end pipelines
to better predict when

00:12:11.230 --> 00:12:13.030
these settlements might fail.

00:12:13.030 --> 00:12:14.470
This has allowed
BNY Mellon to get

00:12:14.470 --> 00:12:16.480
in front of these
potential failures

00:12:16.480 --> 00:12:20.500
and help mitigate them
before the data cutoff.

00:12:20.500 --> 00:12:23.050
Outside of solutions in the
customer experience and risk

00:12:23.050 --> 00:12:26.050
management space, we also
know financial institutions

00:12:26.050 --> 00:12:27.670
want to build
their own AI and ML

00:12:27.670 --> 00:12:30.520
models for a range of different
use cases and business

00:12:30.520 --> 00:12:31.575
problems.

00:12:31.575 --> 00:12:32.950
So at Google Cloud,
we're focused

00:12:32.950 --> 00:12:36.460
on building and deploying
a flexible platform

00:12:36.460 --> 00:12:39.190
to allow financial services
organizations to build out

00:12:39.190 --> 00:12:40.230
these models.

00:12:40.230 --> 00:12:42.190
And this is based on
Google's learnings

00:12:42.190 --> 00:12:44.290
running large-scale ML systems.

00:12:44.290 --> 00:12:48.845
We believe that responsible
AI equals successful AI.

00:12:48.845 --> 00:12:51.520
When we at Google think
about responsible AI,

00:12:51.520 --> 00:12:54.350
we typically think
about four key pillars.

00:12:54.350 --> 00:12:56.170
The first is robustness.

00:12:56.170 --> 00:12:59.862
And that's making sure
results stay robust over time.

00:12:59.862 --> 00:13:01.570
The second is ensuring
that these systems

00:13:01.570 --> 00:13:03.250
are understandable.

00:13:03.250 --> 00:13:06.370
The third is considering
the fairness implications

00:13:06.370 --> 00:13:07.690
of these systems.

00:13:07.690 --> 00:13:10.420
And the fourth is incorporating
security by design

00:13:10.420 --> 00:13:12.550
to keep both the
data and the assets

00:13:12.550 --> 00:13:16.120
secure throughout the
entire model lifecycle.

00:13:16.120 --> 00:13:18.280
In terms of
robustness, at Google,

00:13:18.280 --> 00:13:21.310
we found that transition into
end-to-end machine learning

00:13:21.310 --> 00:13:24.700
pipelines greatly
helps in this way.

00:13:24.700 --> 00:13:28.550
We see that an ML system is
much more than just a model.

00:13:28.550 --> 00:13:32.230
It's about consistently
extracting and validating data,

00:13:32.230 --> 00:13:35.710
ensuring the data distributions
don't shift over time.

00:13:35.710 --> 00:13:38.530
It's about applying the right
features and the right model

00:13:38.530 --> 00:13:42.160
to the problem, training the
model, evaluating the results,

00:13:42.160 --> 00:13:44.680
and ultimately deploying
it to production.

00:13:44.680 --> 00:13:47.800
A common failure mode that we
often hear from our customers

00:13:47.800 --> 00:13:50.930
is a great model that
works well in development,

00:13:50.930 --> 00:13:53.100
but can't transition
to production.

00:13:53.100 --> 00:13:56.030
It doesn't replicate
results in production.

00:13:56.030 --> 00:13:58.150
So we've introduced
AI Platform Pipelines,

00:13:58.150 --> 00:14:00.890
which is coming soon, to
help with this problem.

00:14:00.890 --> 00:14:02.770
AI Platform Pipelines
is integrated

00:14:02.770 --> 00:14:05.530
with other GCP native
services, such as our data

00:14:05.530 --> 00:14:10.117
warehouse, BigQuery, and our AI
serverless training platform.

00:14:10.117 --> 00:14:11.950
We know that financial
institutions are also

00:14:11.950 --> 00:14:15.940
focused on making sure ML
systems are understandable.

00:14:15.940 --> 00:14:18.670
We know that this is key
in financial services

00:14:18.670 --> 00:14:21.880
for a couple key
reasons, the first being

00:14:21.880 --> 00:14:25.780
that these systems often
underpin high-value business

00:14:25.780 --> 00:14:28.510
problems, where the
results need to be robust

00:14:28.510 --> 00:14:30.580
and they need to
be understandable.

00:14:30.580 --> 00:14:32.980
Third, these systems often
interact with humans,

00:14:32.980 --> 00:14:35.890
whether it's in a model
governance approval process

00:14:35.890 --> 00:14:39.950
or in downstream processes, such
as an analyst reviewing a fraud

00:14:39.950 --> 00:14:41.080
alert.

00:14:41.080 --> 00:14:43.120
And finally, we know
these models need

00:14:43.120 --> 00:14:46.000
to meet regulatory obligations.

00:14:46.000 --> 00:14:49.060
So Google has built our
explainable AI solution

00:14:49.060 --> 00:14:51.670
based on Google's experience
understanding and interpreting

00:14:51.670 --> 00:14:53.200
ML systems.

00:14:53.200 --> 00:14:56.920
This solution provides
feature attributions

00:14:56.920 --> 00:15:00.480
to say which attributes or
features are most predictive

00:15:00.480 --> 00:15:02.290
of any given prediction?

00:15:02.290 --> 00:15:04.540
In this hypothetical example
that you see in the lower

00:15:04.540 --> 00:15:07.060
right-hand corner, you can
see that the amount, feature,

00:15:07.060 --> 00:15:12.310
or attribute is most predictive
of this particular prediction.

00:15:12.310 --> 00:15:16.600
Google is also very committed
to AI and ML fairness.

00:15:16.600 --> 00:15:18.430
We've released
our AI principles,

00:15:18.430 --> 00:15:21.700
which govern applications in
which Google applies its AI

00:15:21.700 --> 00:15:23.170
and ML technology.

00:15:23.170 --> 00:15:26.110
And we've also released
a fairness toolkit.

00:15:26.110 --> 00:15:28.390
I want to highlight a
couple of components of it.

00:15:28.390 --> 00:15:30.700
First is the what if
tool, which allows

00:15:30.700 --> 00:15:35.240
organizations to dynamically
dissect their data and results.

00:15:35.240 --> 00:15:37.480
The second is
fairness indicators,

00:15:37.480 --> 00:15:40.720
which allows organizations
to provide categories

00:15:40.720 --> 00:15:42.820
and then slice different
fairness performance

00:15:42.820 --> 00:15:46.060
metrics by those categories
to evaluate differences

00:15:46.060 --> 00:15:47.513
across groups.

00:15:47.513 --> 00:15:49.680
And finally, I want to talk
about our responsible AI

00:15:49.680 --> 00:15:52.900
practices to provide guidelines
to organizations implementing

00:15:52.900 --> 00:15:55.000
these systems.

00:15:55.000 --> 00:15:56.978
Next, I want to
talk about security

00:15:56.978 --> 00:15:59.020
and how at Google Cloud,
we're incorporating this

00:15:59.020 --> 00:16:02.690
by design in our AI
platform and solutions.

00:16:02.690 --> 00:16:04.540
We know security is
absolutely table stakes

00:16:04.540 --> 00:16:06.530
for a financial
services organization.

00:16:06.530 --> 00:16:08.630
So we're focused
on a couple areas.

00:16:08.630 --> 00:16:11.110
The first is preventing
data exfiltration

00:16:11.110 --> 00:16:13.120
with our VPC Service
Controls and ensuring

00:16:13.120 --> 00:16:15.100
no external internet access.

00:16:15.100 --> 00:16:16.790
The second is providing choice.

00:16:16.790 --> 00:16:18.850
So customers can use
their own encryption keys

00:16:18.850 --> 00:16:20.830
to encrypt and decrypt data.

00:16:20.830 --> 00:16:24.040
And customers can choose
where data processing happens

00:16:24.040 --> 00:16:27.190
and where their data is stored
in terms of data residency.

00:16:27.190 --> 00:16:29.770
And finally, always ensuring
authorized and appropriate

00:16:29.770 --> 00:16:33.070
access to that data through
fine grain identity and access

00:16:33.070 --> 00:16:37.990
management controls, as well as
our Access Transparency Logs.

00:16:37.990 --> 00:16:40.480
Finally, I want to turn
to a customer who's

00:16:40.480 --> 00:16:43.840
using Google Cloud's AI
and ML infrastructure.

00:16:43.840 --> 00:16:46.180
Two Sigma has moved some of
their deep learning research

00:16:46.180 --> 00:16:48.460
to the cloud, in
large part to leverage

00:16:48.460 --> 00:16:51.310
the latest AI and
ML infrastructure.

00:16:51.310 --> 00:16:54.130
They're using Google Cloud's
tensor processing units,

00:16:54.130 --> 00:16:57.520
or TPUs, which are
purpose-built chips by Google

00:16:57.520 --> 00:17:00.520
for machine learning
training and inference.

00:17:00.520 --> 00:17:03.400
And you can see when Two
Sigma moved their TensorFlow

00:17:03.400 --> 00:17:06.910
workloads to TPUs, they saw a
boost in productivity, which

00:17:06.910 --> 00:17:09.099
allows them to spend
less time worrying

00:17:09.099 --> 00:17:11.410
about managing their
AI and ML systems

00:17:11.410 --> 00:17:15.050
and more time crafting results.

00:17:15.050 --> 00:17:17.750
In summary, Google
Cloud is focused

00:17:17.750 --> 00:17:20.510
on providing
data-powered innovation

00:17:20.510 --> 00:17:22.460
in financial services.

00:17:22.460 --> 00:17:24.890
We're building out
AI-powered solutions

00:17:24.890 --> 00:17:27.140
in customer experience
and risk management

00:17:27.140 --> 00:17:29.270
for financial services
organizations.

00:17:29.270 --> 00:17:32.960
And we're also focused on
enabling a digital platform

00:17:32.960 --> 00:17:35.120
to allow financial services
organizations to build

00:17:35.120 --> 00:17:38.240
and deploy their own AI and
ML solutions in a way that

00:17:38.240 --> 00:17:40.370
meets the requirements.

00:17:40.370 --> 00:17:42.320
We look forward to
meeting you and working

00:17:42.320 --> 00:17:45.597
with you help transform the
future of financial services.

00:17:45.597 --> 00:17:47.180
If you're interested
in learning more,

00:17:47.180 --> 00:17:49.730
please follow the
URL on the slide.

00:17:49.730 --> 00:17:52.430
Thank you so much for
taking time out of your day.

00:17:52.430 --> 00:17:55.360
And again, we look forward
to working with you.

